<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#middle-persian">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle Persian</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pahlavi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Middle Persian, also called Pahlavi, was the Iranian language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a> and ancestral to modern Persian. It was written in a script derived from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> and used for royal inscriptions, coin legends, administration and Zoroastrian scripture. Papyri in Middle Persian survive from Sasanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the language appears on the coins of the Kushano-Sasanian rulers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/middle-persian.jpg" alt="Middle Persian" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ThatDohDude · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Middle_Persian.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
